## 16. UI/UX Guidelines

- Clean, modern, healthcare-focused visual language that reads as **trustworthy and professional**, not clinical/cold.
- Fully responsive across desktop, tablet, and mobile.
- Consistent use of cards, tables, charts, filters, search bars, and modals across all three applications (Public/Patient, CRM, Admin) for a unified design system.
- Explicit handling of **empty states**, **loading states**, and **error states** on every data view — no blank screens or silent failures.
- Important actions (Book Appointment, Add Patient, Confirm Appointment, Pay Now) are always one click away from the relevant context — avoid burying primary actions in secondary menus.
- Avoid interface complexity: progressive disclosure (tabs, expandable sections) is preferred over dense single-page forms, especially in patient profile and CRM views.

---

## 17. API & Mobile-Readiness Strategy

- All product functionality is exposed via a **versioned REST API** (e.g., `/api/v1/...`), consumed identically by the web frontends.
- No business logic (validation, permission checks, tenant scoping) lives only in the frontend — it is enforced server-side so future native mobile apps (Android/iOS) can integrate directly and securely.
- API responses are designed to be **UI-agnostic** (structured JSON, not pre-formatted for a specific screen), enabling reuse across web and mobile.
- Webhooks/events (appointment status change, payment confirmation) are structured for future push-notification integration on mobile.

